# Code Guidelines and Best Practices

## Verify Information

Always verify information before presenting it. Do not make assumptions or speculate without clear evidence.

## File-by-File Changes

Make changes file by file and give a chance to spot mistakes before proceeding to the next file.

## No Apologies

Never use apologies in code reviews or documentation.

## Understanding Feedback

Avoid giving feedback about understanding in comments or documentation.

## Whitespace Suggestions

Don't suggest whitespace changes unless they're critical to code style.

## Summaries

Don't summarize changes made; instead focus on substantive improvements.

## Avoid Inventions

Don't invent changes other than what's explicitly requested.

## Unnecessary Confirmations

Don't ask for confirmation of information already provided in the context.

## Preserve Existing Code

Don't remove unrelated code or functionalities. Pay attention to preserving existing structures.

## Single Chunk Edits

Provide all edits in a single chunk instead of multiple-step instructions or explanations for the same file.

## Implementation Checks

Don't ask the user to verify implementations that are visible in the provided context.

## Unnecessary Updates

Don't suggest updates or changes to files when there are no actual modifications needed.

## Real File Links

Always provide links to the real files, not context-generated versions.

## Current Implementation

Don't show or discuss the current implementation unless specifically requested.

## Context Generated File Content

Remember to check the context-generated file for current file contents and implementations.

## Use Explicit Variable Names

Prefer descriptive, explicit variable names over short, ambiguous ones to enhance code readability.

- In C#, follow PascalCase for public members and camelCase for local variables and parameters
- Use clear, intent-revealing names that describe purpose
- Avoid single-letter variables except in loops

## Follow Consistent Coding Style

Adhere to the existing coding style in the project for consistency.

- For .NET projects, align with Microsoft's C# coding conventions
- Match the established patterns in the codebase
- Use the same indentation, spacing, and formatting conventions

## Prioritize Performance

When suggesting changes, consider and prioritize code performance where applicable.

- In async scenarios, ensure proper async/await usage and avoid blocking calls
- Consider algorithmic complexity and data structure choices
- Profile code before optimizing
- Don't over-optimize prematurely

## Security-First Approach

Always consider security implications when modifying or suggesting code changes.

- In .NET, utilize nullable reference types and null-safety features
- Validate user input and sanitize data
- Avoid hardcoding secrets or sensitive information
- Follow OWASP guidelines for web applications

## Test Coverage

Suggest or include appropriate unit tests for new or modified code.

- Use xUnit, NUnit, or MSTest conventions depending on the project
- Follow the Arrange-Act-Assert pattern
- Test both success and failure scenarios
- Maintain high code coverage for critical paths

## Error Handling

Implement robust error handling and logging where necessary.

- In C#, create custom exception types and use try-catch at appropriate boundaries
- Log exceptions with full context information
- Provide meaningful error messages to users
- Don't swallow exceptions silently

## Modular Design

Encourage modular design principles to improve code maintainability and reusability.

- Apply SOLID principles, especially dependency injection patterns in .NET
- Keep functions small and focused
- Extract repeated code into reusable functions
- Use composition over inheritance

## Version Compatibility

Ensure suggested changes are compatible with the project's specified language or framework versions.

- For .NET, specify e.g., .NET 6+, C# 10+ where applicable
- Check minimum version requirements for dependencies
- Use appropriate APIs for the target framework
- Document version-specific behavior

## Avoid Magic Numbers

Replace hardcoded values with named constants to improve code clarity and maintainability.

- Use descriptive constant names
- Group related constants together
- Keep constants in a central location

## Consider Edge Cases

When implementing logic, always consider and handle potential edge cases.

- Test with boundary values
- Handle null or empty inputs gracefully
- In LINQ queries and null scenarios, ensure proper null-safety
- Consider off-by-one errors in loops

## Use Assertions

Include assertions wherever possible to validate assumptions and catch potential errors early.

- In .NET, leverage Debug.Assert and unit tests
- Use contract annotations where applicable
- Fail fast on invalid states
- Document assumptions with assertions
